{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>Which they dwelt in Egypt.</b> After the rest of the sojournings that they dwelt as strangers in a land not their own." "<b>Thirty years and four hundred years.</b> All together, from the birth of Yitzchak until now, were four hundred. From when he had seed to Avraham was fulfilled ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״, and thirty years had passed from when the decree of בֵּין הַבְּתָרִים was issued until Yitzchak was born. And it is impossible to say in the land of Egypt alone, for Kehat was among those who came with Yaakov — go and count all his years and all the years of Amram his son and the eighty of Moshe, you will not find them that many. And perforce Kehat had many years before he descended to Egypt, and many of the years of Amram are absorbed in the years of Kehat, and many of the eighty of Moshe are absorbed in the years of Amram — so you will not find four hundred for the entry into Egypt. And you are forced to say perforce that even the other sojournings were called גֵּרוּת, even in Chevron, as it says ״אֲשֶׁר גָר שָׁם אַבְרָהָם וְיִצְחָק״ (Genesis 35), and it says ״אֶת אֶרֶץ מְגֻרֵיהֶם אֲשֶׁר גָרוּ בָהּ״ (Exodus 6). Therefore you must say ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״ — from when he had seed. And when you count 400 years from the birth of Yitzchak, you will find 210 from their entry into Egypt until their departure. And this is one of the things they changed for King Talmai (Megillah 9)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:53.150868873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}